          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Philosophy of Pragmatism</strong><br>\nWilliam James is often referred to as the \"Father of American Psychology,\" but he also made substantial contributions to philosophy. He developed the philosophy of pragmatism, which focuses on the practical effects of ideas and beliefs as the key to their meaning and truth. James argued that the value of any idea is directly related to its usefulness in the practical world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Educational Contributions</strong><br>\nJames's thoughts on education were forward-thinking. He believed in active learning and the importance of adapting teaching to individual student needs. His perspectives on education reflected his pragmatic philosophy, with an emphasis on educational methods that produced practical outcomes and developed critical thinking skills.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Religious and Mystical Interests</strong><br>\nIn addition to his scientific endeavors, James had a deep interest in religion and mysticism. His book \"The Varieties of Religious Experience\" examines different religious experiences from a psychological standpoint. He suggested that religious experiences have profound psychological importance and are as legitimate as any other human experiences.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
