            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Legacy in Psychology</strong><br>\nJames's legacy in psychology is monumental. He introduced new ways of looking at the human psyche, stressing the importance of understanding consciousness and behavior. His teaching and writings influenced many, and his ideas are still taught in modern psychology courses, maintaining their relevance over a century later.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Character</strong><br>\nJames's personal life was marked by struggles with various ailments and depression, yet he maintained a prolific professional life. His character was marked by openness to new ideas and a generous spirit. He married Alice Howe Gibbens in 1878, and they had five children, with whom he shared a close and loving relationship.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Influence</strong><br>\nWilliam James died on August 26, 1910, leaving behind a rich legacy of thought and scholarship. His influence extended beyond psychology and philosophy into other domains, such as education and religion. His death marked the loss of a luminary who contributed to shaping modern thought, and his work continues to inspire students and scholars around the world.</p>\n"
              },
